\section{Statistical Analysis and Prespecified Criteria}\label{app:prospective}
\subsection{Effect magnitude and statistical interpretation}
Write $\widehat\Delta=\widehat\theta_{\Vis}$ and $\widehat\Gamma=\widehat\theta_{\Com}$. Before inference, the analysis plan set a ten-percentage-point magnitude benchmark. It combined $\widehat\Delta\leq-.10$ with a six-comparison upper interval endpoint below zero for completion cost, and $\widehat\Gamma\geq+.10$ with a lower endpoint above zero for common-target benefit. A joint magnitude interpretation required both conditions in at least two models. Models with fewer than 12 visible successes over 240 outputs were designated support-limited.

Qwen and Olmo show common-nine changes of 7.50 and 9.17 percentage points, with adjusted intervals $[1.67,14.17]$ and $[3.33,16.67]$. Both intervals exclude zero and span the predeclared ten-point benchmark. Both point estimates fall below that magnitude criterion. We evaluate the $H_2$ target with its specified three-model comparison family.

Marginal 95\% intervals for visible/common differences are $[-55.00,-37.50]$/$[3.33,12.50]$ for Qwen, $[-40.83,-20.83]$/$[4.17,15.00]$ for Olmo, and $[-6.67,1.67]$/$[0,2.50]$ for corrected Phi. The main primary table retains the original six-comparison intervals.

\subsection{Exact paired-binary sensitivity}
We also compute exact paired-binary tests for the six original contrasts. Let $g$ and $\ell$ count separation-only and control-only successes. Under independent paired sampling and paired marginal symmetry, $g\mid(g+\ell)$ is $\operatorname{Binomial}(g+\ell,1/2)$. We use a two-sided exact tail test and $\min(1,6p)$ \citep{mcnemar1947,dror2018}. These tests complement the planned percentile bootstrap.

